  "query_preview": "/Through Wednesday/...\nIssued at 133 AM MDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nGenerally dry southwesterly flow aloft continues today. The main\nplayers of the week - a strong closed 500mb low over the Pacific\nNorthwest, and a broad ridge centered across the \ncentral/southeastern CONUS - won't do much over the next 24-48\nhours. For Colorado, that means most of the state will remain\nrelatively quiet over the next couple of days. Mid-level\ntemperatures near 14-17C today and tomorrow will likely lead to\nhigh temperatures in the low 90s. The overall synoptic pattern\nfavors the development of a Denver cyclone today, and there may be\nsome minor temperature gradients across the metro depending on\nwhere that sets up. While a couple of CAMs and REFS members\nattempt to develop a shower or two near the convergence zone of\nthe cyclone this evening, that probability remains rather low\n(~10%). Friday looks to be a similar story, though we should start\nto see some gradual moisture advection into the region by the\nafternoon and evening, leading to some scattered showers and\nthunderstorms across the higher terrain... especially along/south\nof I-70.\n\nAs we approach the weekend, the ridge is expected to build across\nthe Central Great Plains, while the upper trough remains nearly\nstationary over western Washington/Oregon through at least Sunday.\nThat gives us a couple of days of fairly well aligned southerly or\nsouth-southwesterly flow aloft, which should allow for a fairly\nrobust plume of moisture to stream into the region... perhaps even\nadvecting in some moisture from the now-Hurricane Marie. While the\nincrease in moisture should allow for widespread showers and\nstorms across the higher terrain, the absence of any meaningful \nsynoptic scale forcing will likely limit the amount of \nprecipitation across the plains, where thunderstorm chances would \nbe driven by a myriad of mesoscale influences.\n\nMoisture is expected to hang around through Monday, but the ridge\nis expected to expand a bit further west by sometime early next\nweek, shifting the flow aloft towards the west-southwest. Forecast\nuncertainty quickly increases by mid-week. Across the 00z ensemble\nsuite, it's close to a 50/50 split on maintaining a good moisture\nfeed into the state. However, there is a rather robust signal for\nabove normal temperatures to continue for the foreseeable future.\nThe last couple cycles of the ECMWF (along with the AIGEFS and\nECMWF AIFS ensemble) keep high temperatures in the 80s to low 90s\nover the next several days, with no real hint of a substantial\npattern change anytime in the next 7-10 days.",
